/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  flowsheetDict --- ED08 tartaric stabilisation of a red wine, on a CONDUCTIVITY target
  ===========================================================================

  ETHANOL IS NOT IN THIS WINE, AND THAT IS THE FIRST THING TO KNOW.  A new
  medium red carries about 13.5 % v/v of ethanol (106 g/L), and the stack
  cannot be told so: every non-water component passes through the aqueous
  species bridge, and declaring `ethanol` in the components list REFUSES
  ("aqueousMapping: component 'ethanol' declares no aqueous mapping" --
  measured on a twin of this case; an ethanol given a mapping would then
  refuse for having no species row and no D0).  So the engine prices the
  solvent as PURE WATER: Re and Sc on water's viscosity, every ion's
  conductivity on its infinite-dilution D0 in water.  How much that costs
  was COMPUTED with the engine's own liquid-viscosity route, not cited: a
  `propertyScan1D` of `viscosity_liquid` on {water 0.955, ethanol 0.045}
  (13.5 % v/v) against pure water, Vogel per component and the package's
  Grunberg-Nissan mixing rule, gives mu_mix/mu_water = 1.0094e-3 / 1.0011e-3
  = 1.0084 at 293.15 K -- 0.8 %.  That is the number the engine has, and it
  is a lower bound on the truth: the rule is ln mu = sum x_i ln mu_i with NO
  binary term, so it interpolates between the two pure viscosities and can
  never sit above both, while a water-ethanol mixture can.  Mobilities and
  conductivities scale with 1/mu, so kappa_in and kappa_out are both
  overestimated by whatever the real factor is; the RELATIVE target (20 %)
  is insensitive to a common factor, the ABSOLUTE stack voltage and power
  are not.  NAMED AND NOT BUILT: a neutral co-solvent carried through the
  mass balance and priced into the solvent viscosity (the design record's
  NOT DONE).  Everything below stands on that caveat.

  THE TARGET IS CONDUCTIVITY, BECAUSE THAT IS WHAT IS MEASURED.  Vitor
  Geraldes framed it in one sentence -- "na eletrodialise aponta para a
  condutividade, porque isso e que e medido" -- and the tartaric-stability
  practice on wine is stated as a per-cent DROP in the wine's electrical
  conductivity (a 20 % tartrate removal "is considered sufficient to
  stabilize red wines": Tsygurina et al., Membranes 12 (2022) 1187, citing
  Ribereau-Gayon et al., Handbook of Enology vol. 2, 2nd ed., 2006 -- the
  handbook itself was not reached; the 20 % here is also Vitor's own
  operating practice on this very unit, origin measured, reviewStatus
  unverified).  So this case is the first to use the third operating form:

      stack                      EurodiaED-100P-50;   // his 100-cell-pair, 50 m2 unit
      membrane                   CMX_AMX;             // the record names no pair; the CASE must
      targetConductivityRemoval  0.20;                // 20 % of kappa, and Choupo solves I

  `targetDemin` would have fixed the demineralisation of the FIRST cation in
  component order (K here) and moved with the order of the components list;
  the conductivity target counts every ion with its own z^2 D0 c at both
  ends of the channel and does not care how the list is written.  The
  gate (check_ed_stack arm (m)) holds that with a reordered twin.

  THE WINE: a typical new medium red, built from PUBLISHED RANGES.  One
  coherent primary for the ions, READ in full (open access):
      Siren, Siren & Siren, "Evaluation of organic and inorganic compounds
      levels of red wines processed from Pinot Noir grapes", Analytical
      Chemistry Research 3 (2015) 26-36, Table 2 -- eight bottled Pinot Noir
      red wines, 12.5-14.5 % alcohol, pH 3.51-4.02 at 20 C (measured).
  Cross-checked, not blended, against two more readable sources: Jakkielska
  et al., Foods 12 (2023) 3205 (22 red wines, ICP-OES, K mean 1521 mg/L) and
  USDA FoodData Central SR Legacy 173190 "Alcoholic beverage, wine, table,
  red" (public domain: K 127, Ca 8, Mg 12, Na 4, P 23 mg per 100 g; ethanol
  10.6 g/100 g).  Values chosen INSIDE the Siren range (wine 6, whose
  tartaric acid reads 10 mg/L, excluded as anomalous for the acids):

      quantity          chosen       Siren 2015 range       other readable source
      -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
      K+                1000 mg/L    740 - 1077             1521 (Jakkielska), ~1260 (USDA)
      Na+                120 mg/L    99.5 - 181             ~40 (USDA)
      Ca2+                50 mg/L    34.3 - 59.7            ~80 (USDA); 41.9 mean (Gajek 2021, n=79)
      Mg2+               100 mg/L    69.2 - 128.0           ~120 (USDA); 57.1 mean (Gajek 2021)
      Cl-                 40 mg/L    25.3 - 54.3            --
      phosphate (as HPO4) 650 mg/L   463 - 1150             USDA P 23 mg/100 g = ~700 mg/L as HPO4
      tartaric acid     1800 mg/L    1427 - 2177            Tsygurina 2022 model red wine 0.013 M (1950 mg/L)
      malic acid         250 mg/L    101 - 447              (malolactic wines)
      lactic acid       2300 mg/L    1382 - 3362            (malolactic wines)
      pH                 3.60        3.51 - 4.02 (20 C)     3.33 mean, 2.53-4.60 (Gajek 2021, n=79)
      ethanol            13.5 % v/v  12.5 - 14.5            USDA 10.6 g/100 g = ~13.3 % v/v
      density            993 g/L     --                     Bica et al., Molecules 25 (2020) 2961 (measured wine)
      sulfate            CLOSURE     21 - 53 (IC)           see ELECTRONEUTRALITY below

  Gajek et al., Molecules 26 (2021) 214, is quoted for Ca, Mg and pH only:
  its K mean for red wines (72 mg/L) is an order of magnitude below every
  other survey and is not used.  Succinic and acetic acid (Siren: 534-853 and
  605-1297 mg/L) are NOT declared: with pKa 4.207 (succinic, Pinching &
  Bates, quoted in Eden & Bates 1959 Table 6) a unit above the wine pH they
  are mostly undissociated and would add of the order of 2 meq/L of anions
  -- named here as part of what the closure below absorbs.

  SPECIATION AT THE FEED pH IS FIXED AND DECLARED.  The steady stack does
  not re-speciate, so the ionic forms are computed once, here, with
  thermodynamic pKa in WATER at 25 C from primaries read in full (all NIST
  Journal of Research, public domain):
      tartaric  pK1 3.036  pK2 4.366   Bates & Canham, J. Res. NBS 47 (1951) 343
      malic     pK1 3.459  pK2 5.097   Eden & Bates,   J. Res. NBS 62 (1959) 161
      phosphoric pK1 2.148 pK2 7.200   Bates, J. Res. NBS 47 (1951) 127; Bates & Acree,
                                       J. Res. NBS 30 (1943) 129 (pK2 = 1979.5/T - 5.3541 + 0.019840 T)
      lactic    pKa 3.862             Nims & Smith, J. Biol. Chem. 113 (1936) 145, AS QUOTED by
                                       Mauger, Dissolution Technol. 24(3) (2017) 38 -- primary not reached
  Henderson-Hasselbalch on a diprotic acid at pH 3.60:
      r1 = 10^(pH - pK1),  r2 = 10^(pH - pK2),  D = 1 + r1 + r1 r2
      alpha0 = 1/D (neutral),  alpha1 = r1/D (mono-anion),  alpha2 = r1 r2/D (di-anion)
      tartaric:  r1 3.664  r2 0.1714  -> alpha0 0.1889  alpha1 0.6924  alpha2 0.1187
      malic:     r1 1.383  r2 0.0318  -> alpha0 0.4119  alpha1 0.5699  alpha2 0.0181
      phosphoric: alpha1(H2PO4-) 0.9657, H3PO4 0.0341, HPO4 2- 0.00024 (dropped)
      lactic:    alpha = 1/(1 + 10^(pKa - pH)) = 0.3536
  applied to 11.993 mmol/L tartaric, 1.8645 malic, 25.533 lactic and 6.772
  phosphate (as HPO4) gives the anions in 0/diluateFeed.  The NEUTRAL forms
  cannot be declared (a neutral molecule has no species row and carries no
  current) and are LEFT OUT of the feed: 18.9 % of the tartaric acid
  (2.27 mmol/L H2Tart), 41.2 % of the malic (0.77 mmol/L), 64.6 % of the
  lactic (16.5 mmol/L) and 3.4 % of the phosphate.  For a CONDUCTIVITY
  target that is the right omission -- a neutral carries no charge -- and
  the consequence is the one to remember: the stack removes IONS, and the
  undissociated acid left behind re-dissociates as the pH drifts, which this
  fixed-speciation case cannot see.  The pKa are the WATER values: in a
  13.5 % ethanol medium they shift upward (Berg & Keefer 1958 determined
  the tartaric constants "in alcohol" and found their ratio differs; the
  numbers were not read), so alpha1 of tartaric is somewhat overstated here.
  H+ is declared at c = 10^-pH = 0.2512 mmol/L, taking a_H = c_H (announced
  simplification; Davies puts gamma about 0.85 at this ionic strength).

  ELECTRONEUTRALITY IS THE AUTHOR'S TO CLOSE, and the engine does not check
  it (there is no electroneutrality test in ElectrodialysisStack.cpp --
  measured).  With everything above:
      cations  K 25.577 + Na 5.220 + 2 Ca 2.495 + 2 Mg 8.229 + H 0.251 = 41.772 meq/L
      anions   HTart 8.304 + 2 Tart 2.846 + HMal 1.063 + 2 Mal 0.068 + Lactate 9.028
             + Cl 1.128 + H2PO4 6.540                                   = 28.977 meq/L
      gap                                                                12.795 meq/L
  closed on ONE named ion, SULFATE: 12.795/2 = 6.397 mmol/L = 614.5 mg/L
  SO4 2- (1.115 g/L expressed as K2SO4).  Why sulfate: Siren's own table
  contradicts itself on it -- its ion-chromatography sulfate (20.6-53.3 mg/L)
  sits against a total-sulfur column of 99-252 mg S/L (363-755 mg/L if all
  of it were sulfate), and the paper notes matrix interference on the
  sulfate peak; it is the one measured anion whose value the source itself
  puts in doubt by an order of magnitude.  The closure lands inside that
  total-sulfur ceiling and JUST ABOVE the OIV maximum acceptable limit for
  a young dry wine (1 g/L as K2SO4; OIV International Code of Oenological
  Practices, Annex "Maximum acceptable limits"), which says plainly that not
  all of the missing anionic charge is sulfate -- some is the succinate and
  acetate not declared, bisulfite adducts and phenolate the analysis did not
  measure.  The magnitude is stated so a reader can move it: this is the
  `aqueousAnalysis` posture (a named adjustment of a stated size), never a
  silent one.

  EVERY ION NEEDS A D0, AND SIX OF THESE HAD NONE.  `electrolyte::ionD0`
  refuses an ion without a `D0` row -- correctly -- and the limiting-current
  PREDICTION (GeraldesAfonso2010) runs only when every diluate ion resolves
  one.  K, Na, Ca, Mg, H, Cl and SO4 carry curated D0 in data/standards/
  species/; HTart, Tart, H2PO4 do not, and HMal, Mal, Lactate have no species
  record at all.  They are supplied CASE-LOCALLY under constant/species/,
  each `origin estimated; reviewStatus unverified;` with the method and a
  note naming the primary to verify against (none of those primaries --
  Marsh, Spiro & Selvaratnam 1963 for bitartrate; Bester-Rogac, Neueder,
  Barthel & Apelblat 1997/1998 for the tartrates; Selvaratnam & Spiro 1965
  and Pethybridge et al. 2006 for H2PO4- -- could be read here, and a value
  written from memory would be an invisible falsehood).  The method, from
  facts the tree already carries: Stokes-Einstein at 25 C on the engine's
  own water viscosity (8.8987e-4 Pa s, the Vogel fit of water.dat) with the
  bitartrate radius taken as the sphere of its CRYSTAL molar volume --
  potassium bitartrate's rho_p 1980 kg/m3 and MW 188.177 give 95.04 cm3/mol,
  minus K+'s conventional V0 9.02 cm3/mol = 86.02 cm3/mol, r = 3.243e-10 m,
  D0 = 7.568e-10 m2/s (lambda0 28.4 S cm2/mol by Nernst-Einstein); Tart 2-
  the same radius; HMal, Mal and Lactate scaled by the cube root of their
  molar-mass ratio to bitartrate (0.9629, 0.9604, 0.8422); H2PO4- set equal
  to the isosteric sulfate ion's curated 1.06e-9.  The engine ANNOUNCES each
  of the six on every run ([estimate] species ..., the caveat block, the
  result JSON) -- the announcement was ADDED for this case, because the
  species bridge dropped the provenance block and an estimated D0 was being
  read in the same silence as a measured one.  The case-local route was
  VERIFIED to win over the catalogue before any of this was trusted (a
  deliberately wrong D0 in constant/species/HTart.dat moved kappa; the
  catalogue's own HTart.dat has no D0 to shadow).

  THE BRINE.  The concentrate loop of a wine ED plant collects the salt the
  stack moves, and that salt is sparingly soluble: 0/concentrateFeed is a
  potassium bitartrate solution at 20 mmol/L (3.76 g/L), 76 % of KHT's
  solubility in WATER at 20 C (4.92 g/L: Berg & Keefer 1958 Table 1, as
  reproduced by the AWRI's potassium-instability page -- the primary was not
  reached), because that ceiling is why such a loop is bled.  Measured, not
  argued, on a twin with the brine at 10 mmol/L: I is IDENTICAL (1.4583 A --
  the conductivity target is a property of the diluate alone) while U falls
  from 5.476 to 3.134 V, through the membrane back-EMF (E_mem +0.019 ->
  -0.014 V per pair) and the concentrate channel's resistance (0.0068 ->
  0.0135 Ohm per pair).  The concentrate enters the diluate's answer
  nowhere else.

  WHAT A STUDENT SHOULD READ OFF THE LOG -- and the two findings this feed
  exposes about the UNIT.

  (1) THE ANSWER.  kappa_in 0.5003 S/m -> kappa_out 0.4002 S/m (20.00 %),
      I = 1.458 A on 0.5 m2 per cell pair (i = 2.92 A/m2), U = 5.48 V,
      P = 8.0 W, 0.0027 kWh/m3 of wine; i_lim = 207.4 A/m2 (GeraldesAfonso2010,
      set by the anion-exchange membrane, D corrected to 20 C through
      Stokes-Einstein with factor 0.874), so i/i_lim = 0.014 -- Vitor's
      machine at 20 % removal on this wine is nowhere near its limiting
      current on THIS model.  The Newton converges in 2 iterations from the
      linear seed (kappa_out is linear in I until a cap binds).

  (2) THE FEED'S CONDUCTIVITY IS TOO HIGH, and the run says why.  5.0 mS/cm,
      where instruments read 1.5-3 mS/cm on such wines (Tsygurina et al.'s
      model red-wine solution: 2.04 mS/cm at pH 3.25).  The engine's kappa
      is sum c_i lambda_i with lambda at INFINITE DILUTION (no Kohlrausch
      relaxation or electrophoretic term at I ~ 0.05 mol/kg), on PURE WATER
      (no ethanol, first paragraph), with D0 at its 25 C reference divided
      by RT at the run T (a 1/T that goes the wrong way: the Stokes-Einstein
      correction the limiting current receives is NOT applied to kappa --
      an engine inconsistency, named in the design record and not fixed
      here because it moves every ED golden), and with the sulfate closure
      above.  A relative target is insensitive to a common factor; U and P
      are not.

  (3) THE UNIT'S FARADAY SPLIT IS A SINGLE-SALT IDEALISATION, AND A
      13-ION WINE SHOWS IT.  Every counter-ion loses xi I N/(|z| F) mol/s
      REGARDLESS of its concentration or mobility: 1.632 mmol/L from each
      monovalent ion and 0.816 from each divalent one at this current.  So
      K+ falls 6.4 % (25.58 -> 23.94 mmol/L) while H+, Cl-, HMal- and Mal 2-
      are removed ENTIRELY -- the cap binds four times and the [WARNING]
      says so -- and the diluate leaves with no chloride and no free
      hydrogen ion.  Charge is not conserved either: 5 cations each lose
      xi I N/F equivalents and 8 anions each lose the same, so the diluate
      OUTLET carries +3.64 meq/L of net positive charge (+5.5 % of its
      66.3 eq/L of ion equivalents; the concentrate the mirror -3.64) -- the
      finding CLAUDE.md records as "not charge-balanced on a feed of mixed
      valence", measured at 1.8 % on ed04's three ions and at 5.5 % here on
      thirteen.  RESERVED for Vitor and NOT fixed here (it moves ed04's
      golden).  What a transport-number split would give is a hand
      calculation from the run's OWN printed numbers: sharing the cation
      current by the film limiting transport numbers the engine prints
      (t_lim K 0.746, Na 0.103, Mg 0.086, H 0.035, Ca 0.029) and the anion
      current likewise (SO4 0.337, Lactate 0.202, H2PO4 0.172, HTart 0.156,
      Cl 0.057, Tart 0.054, HMal 0.021), the same 20 % of kappa needs
      I = 6.68 A -- 4.6 times the equal-share answer -- at i/i_lim = 0.064.
      The right number for a real wine lies with the second model; this
      case prints the first because that is what the unit does today, and
      it prints it loudly.

  (4) THE MOTIVE, QUANTIFIED WITH ITS APPROXIMATIONS.  A new wine is
      supersaturated in KHT and will throw crystals in the bottle.  Berg &
      Keefer's Table 1 (via the AWRI) gives KHT's solubility at 20 C as
      2.77 g/L in 12 % and 2.51 g/L in 14 % ethanol; interpolated to 13.5 %,
      2.575 g/L = 13.68 mmol/L.  A saturated KHT solution sits at pH
      (pK1+pK2)/2 = 3.70, where alpha1 = 0.698, so its concentration product
      Ksp' = [K+][HT-] = 13.68 x 9.55 = 130.7 mM2 (water pKa, no activity
      correction, both stated).  The feed: 25.58 x 8.30 = 212.4 mM2, a
      supersaturation ratio S = 1.62.  After the run's 20 % conductivity
      removal: 23.94 x 6.67 = 159.7, S = 1.22 -- still supersaturated by
      this arithmetic, because the equal-share split takes only 6.4 % of the
      potassium.  Under the transport-number split of (3), K falls to about
      20.0 and HT- to about 7.1 mmol/L: S = 1.09.  Neither clears 1.0 on
      paper, and Berg & Keefer themselves report that KHT precipitates far
      more slowly from wine than from alcohol-water (colloids), which is why
      the trade's criterion is the mini-contact test and not a solubility
      product.  The potassiumBitartrate.dat c_sat(T) coefficients are NOT
      used: their own header calls them illustrative.

  (5) TEMPERATURE.  293.15 K (20 C) is declared: ED tartaric stabilisation
      runs at cellar-to-ambient temperature, Siren et al. measured their pH
      at 20 C, and it is the temperature of Geraldes & Afonso's Table 3, so
      the Stokes-Einstein path (factor 0.874 on every D) is exercised where
      ed04 already exercises it.  Re = 55.9, inside the record's 50-86 band.

      diluateFeed (the wine) ->  +-----------------------+ -> diluate (stabilised wine)
                                 | EurodiaED-100P-50     |
      concentrateFeed (brine) -> | 100 pairs, CMX/AMX    | -> concentrate
                                 +-----------------------+
                                          | (power, energy wire)
                                          v
                                     rectifier (electricLoad)
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        edStack;
        type        electrodialysisStack;
        inputs      ( diluateFeed  concentrateFeed );
        outputs     ( diluate  concentrate );

        operation
        {
            stack                      EurodiaED-100P-50;   // the HARDWARE, as a record (Vitor's own unit)
            membrane                   CMX_AMX;             // the record names no pair -- the CASE must
            targetConductivityRemoval  0.20;                // 20 % of the wine's conductivity, and Choupo solves I
            xi                         0.9;                 // current efficiency (the announced default, declared)
            E_electrodes               2.0;                 // V (raw SI) -- lumped electrode overpotential (announced)
        }

        energyOutputs
        (
            { name power;  kind work;  expression "W_electric";  unit W; }
        );
    }
    {
        name        rectifier;
        type        electricLoad;
        operation   { eta 1.0; }
        energyInputs
        (
            { from edStack.power;  kind work;  target W_shaft; }
        );
    }
);
